# Adiona-TV kiosk: input device policy.
#
# THIS FILE USED TO MAKE libinput IGNORE POINTER DEVICES, and no longer does.
#
# The reason it did was cursor visibility: cage paints a cursor for any pointer
# it can see, a cursor parked in one spot for hours is exactly the burn-in the
# screensaver exists to prevent, and CSS cannot reach what the compositor
# composites. Ignoring the device removed the pointer and the cursor with it.
#
# That answer cost more than it bought. It could never cover a combo
# keyboard+trackpad (a Logitech K400, the usual kiosk remote), which reports
# ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD and ID_INPUT_MOUSE on ONE event node — dropping it would take
# the keyboard with it, so the guard below had to let it through and a cursor
# appeared anyway. And it broke the case that now matters: a plain USB or
# Bluetooth mouse, which the settings panel is meant to be usable with.
#
# Cursor visibility is handled where it belongs instead, in two layers:
#
#   * web/index.html sets `cursor: none` on html/body, so the waiting screen and
#     the screensaver never ask for one, and draws the settings panel's pointer
#     from a data: URI image so it is always visible there and nowhere else.
#   * blank-cursor.py + XCURSOR_THEME give the compositor a fully transparent
#     cursor, which covers what the page cannot: the video player's surface.
#
# Nothing is ignored here now. The file is kept, rather than deleted, because the
# reasoning above is worth having next to the box that behaves this way — and
# because a future device that genuinely must be ignored belongs here.
